00:16:18.516 --> 00:16:23.796
The problem that many face is bridging
the gap between data and wisdom.

00:16:24.196 --> 00:16:30.956
A lot of companies face this issue where they have a lot of data,
but they are unable to get actionable insights out of it.

00:16:31.636 --> 00:16:36.116
What we have built with Popular is to bridge
this gap between data and wisdom.

00:16:38.316 --> 00:16:42.116
The vision that SUP S-Team built towards had four pillars.

00:16:42.676 --> 00:16:48.396
Firstly, a single source of truth.
Because when the whole business work from one source for data,

00:16:48.916 --> 00:16:55.356
decisions get faster and trust goes up For Popular,
that meant unifying their finance,

00:16:55.556 --> 00:17:03.036
retail and operations data into one single
business platform. Next self-service insights.

00:17:03.396 --> 00:17:09.516
This means that there is no bottleneck in the tech,
and that people can get answers directly from their data.

00:17:10.756 --> 00:17:17.196
Real time visibility. We're always on dashboards
means that you're acting on what's happening now and not.

00:17:17.236 --> 00:17:20.516
What about happen to your data in the in the past.

00:17:20.516 --> 00:17:27.036
And lastly, a scalable foundation,
because what they have built is able to handle their data today.

00:17:27.276 --> 00:17:31.556
But it also meant to handle the growth
as the business grow in the future.

00:17:33.076 --> 00:17:36.196
And here's what does the heavy lifting on AWS.

00:17:36.756 --> 00:17:40.756
Three main services and each with a distinct skill set.

00:17:41.796 --> 00:17:45.916
Firstly, Amazon S3 is a data lake solution.

00:17:46.276 --> 00:17:55.916
It holds all of historical and incremental data from SAP,
ERP retail source to the POS retail systems across hundred stores.

00:17:57.196 --> 00:18:01.276
This allows for scalable, cost effective solution.

00:18:02.476 --> 00:18:07.636
Next, Amazon Redshift is a cloud data warehouse that Popular is using.

00:18:08.316 --> 00:18:13.276
This allows for single source of truth,
which allows for high performance computing.

00:18:14.836 --> 00:18:18.156
Amazon Quick allows for the layer that sits on top.

00:18:18.876 --> 00:18:25.476
This is where they can build dashboards for executives
and through QuickSight and with the generative AI layer on top,

00:18:25.716 --> 00:18:30.276
users can now ask questions in natural
language and get answers instantly.

00:18:31.756 --> 00:18:34.396
The story isn't these individual services.

00:18:34.676 --> 00:18:36.956
It's how we actually build this together.

00:18:38.956 --> 00:18:42.236
So let me walk you through the architectural step by step.

00:18:43.116 --> 00:18:45.716
First, we have the corporate data center.

00:18:45.716 --> 00:18:51.716
We host SAP ERP data as well as the POS data across the retail system.

00:18:52.676 --> 00:18:56.196
We bring the data into AWS into two mechanisms.

00:18:56.516 --> 00:19:05.556
Firstly, we have the SAP glue SAP data connector,
which brings in data from SAP via the OData API.

00:19:06.756 --> 00:19:15.876
This allows you to join your data in SAP
with other data sources such as retail transactions, operations,

00:19:15.996 --> 00:19:20.156
and this allows you to build a unified view across your data sources.

00:19:21.236 --> 00:19:29.076
Next, we use AWS Database Migration Service
that brings data in from the POS retail transaction.

00:19:29.796 --> 00:19:38.116
This allows for bulk incremental bulk loads
into AWS as well as incremental load so that data stays updated.

00:19:39.756 --> 00:19:43.876
Everything lands in Amazon S3 as the central data lake.

00:19:44.436 --> 00:19:48.116
And this is a key design principle that we have built today.

00:19:48.116 --> 00:19:57.076
This allows you to scale independently when your compute and storage
are completely decoupled and you pay for only what you use.

00:19:58.316 --> 00:20:00.000
Data is also stored in Apache Iceberg Open table format,
and this is a very important concept, which I'll explain on later.

